density_jump
============

The two-dimensional density-jump test of List et al. 2019 (MNRAS 489, 4217), their section 5.1. It
is the direct comparison of the donor and receiver methods: the same dark matter distribution feeds
the same gas, and what differs is only which side of the pair does the neighbour search. Their
result was that the receiver method coupled 17.3 per cent of the energy the donor method did. Ours
is 99.2 per cent, and that difference is the point of running it.

The setup is hostile to the receiver method by construction. Gas fills a regular grid with a factor
1e4 jump in cell mass across x = 20 kpc, so the cells straddling it have wildly different densities.
Dark matter fills a finer regular grid with masses following a two-dimensional Gaussian of width
3 kpc centred on the jump, so its mass is concentrated in a handful of particles while every
particle still has gas in its kernel. The receiver method has to rebuild that peaked mass
distribution by summing kernels at the gas positions; the donor method never needs the dark matter
density at all, because PBH evaporation is one-body and a donor's power depends on its own mass.

Self-gravity is off, as it is in the paper, so nothing moves the gas but the evaporation heating.
The gas is cold enough (0.01 (km/s)^2, a sound speed of 0.1 km/s) that the pressure jump across the
discontinuity drifts it by well under one cell over the whole run: the two no-PBHEF runs finish with
a maximum speed of 0.04 km/s under MFM and 0.16 km/s under MFV, against 9.6 km/s with the heating on.

Files
-----
  make_ics.py          writes the IC; the box size and the softening in the parameters follow from it
  density_jump.params  GIZMO parameters; run_matrix.sh overrides OutputDir per run
  run_matrix.sh        builds and runs the eight configurations
  analyse.py           the table below and runs/density_jump.png
  runs/                everything generated, one directory per configuration

Running
-------
From the repository root:

  python3 scripts/test_problems/outputs/density_jump/make_ics.py
  scripts/test_problems/outputs/density_jump/run_matrix.sh 4
  python3 scripts/test_problems/outputs/density_jump/analyse.py

make_ics.py needs numpy and h5py; analyse.py also needs matplotlib. Neither is needed to run
the simulations themselves, only to build the initial conditions and read the results.

The eight configurations are the two hydro solvers crossed with no PBHEF, PBHEF=1 (receiver),
PBHEF=2 (donor, which weights by the solid angle of their eq. 6 by default), and PBHEF=2 with
PBHEF_MASS_WEIGHTS (donor, the mass-weighted kernel of their eq. 5). The paper uses
MFM. One configuration takes about 70 seconds on four tasks: 258 sync points to t = 1, of which the
129 that cpu.txt records are the ones where the longest timebin is active.

Choosing the normalisation
--------------------------
PBH_MassFraction and PBH_InitialMass are set so the blast stays inside the box for the whole run,
which is the paper's own criterion. A 1e16 g black hole with f = 0.005 radiates 1.3e40 erg/s over
1e10 Msun of dark matter, 4.0e56 erg over the 978.5 Myr of the run, which puts the shock at 11 kpc
under MFM and 13 to 15 kpc under MFV against a half-width of 20 kpc. That mass loses 4e-6 of itself over
the run, so m(t) is effectively constant here and the mass evolution is not part of what this tests;
the cosmo_test problem covers that.

Results
-------
Ratio of the energy the gas received to the analytic total the black holes radiated, and the
instantaneous power coupled against the same total at the start and end of the run:

  config              injected/expected    power t=0  power t=end
  mfm_pbhef1                0.991448704     0.999267     0.963362
  mfm_pbhef2_mass           0.999925270     0.999996     1.000005
  mfm_pbhef2_iso            0.999809803     0.999996     1.000005
  mfv_pbhef1                0.992532974     0.999267     0.973901
  mfv_pbhef2_mass           1.000074580     0.999996     1.000593
  mfv_pbhef2_iso            0.999861159     0.999996     1.000409

The ratio is read at the last sync point the donors deposited on, which for this problem is the final
one, where every timebin is synchronised. Read at an intermediate sync point it lags by up to one
timestep's worth of energy for whichever cells are asleep, which is an artefact of comparing a
continuous expectation against a total that accrues in per-cell chunks, not a loss.

The donor method couples the analytic total to 8e-5 over the run, whichever weighting and whichever
solver. The receiver method starts within 0.07 per cent on the undisturbed grid and degrades to 3.7 per
cent short by the end, as its own feedback evacuates the gas around the dark matter peak and leaves the
peak sampled by a handful of very large cells; integrated over the run that is 0.85 per cent. The
receiver therefore couples 99.2 per cent of what the donor does, against 17.3 per cent in the paper.

Their 82.7 per cent shortfall follows from annihilation being two-body: the receiver method needs
rho_chi^2, and a kernel average suppresses sharp mass peaks quadratically. Evaporation is one-body, so
what the receiver needs is rho_chi, and the volume-weighted sum of a kernel-smoothed density is a
decent quadrature of the total mass as long as the gas samples the peak. The residual is that
quadrature failing where the gas has been blown away, which is what the gas_poor problem isolates.

The two weightings agree on the total to 1e-4 but not on where the energy goes: the mass-weighted
kernel puts 46.5 per cent of the power on the low-density side at t=0 against the solid angle's
50.0 per cent, because a donor whose kernel straddles the jump sees 1e4 times more mass on the dense
side. Only donors within their own kernel radius of the jump can do this, and 16 gas neighbours at
this resolution is a third of a kpc against a 3 kpc peak, which is why the asymmetry comes out at a
few per cent and not a factor of two.
